# Whether anybody looked for a dependence, which is the other half of C. One row per filing.
#
# Matrix and search are one pair
#   A matrix and its search are filed as a pair, and emitting one of them is the failure this model
#   exists to refuse. `entries/couplings.sqlc` says it in its own header: a filing with no couplings
#   has either a stack of independent layers or nobody who looked, and those are opposite claims.
#   Most documents in this corpus state no coupling, so a diagram drawing only the matrix is silent
#   about them in a way a reader will resolve as independence.
#
# A diagram is the worst place for that
#   An absence is invisible on a page in a way it is not in a table. A missing row can be counted;
#   a missing line cannot be seen. That is `examples/diagramming/main.rs`'s own demand, *what could
#   not be drawn must be enumerated*, applied to the corpus instead of to the notation.
#
# The typed answer, not the note
#   The four reasons are the fact: `unmeasured` is nobody looked, `none` is somebody looked and
#   found nothing, `notApplicable` is there is no pair to couple, and a NULL is somebody looked and
#   the couplings are the ones drawn. The note is the argument for the answer, it is free prose,
#   and diagrams/dependences.sqlc says why prose does not travel into an artifact with no unit on
#   it.
#
# Carried on the laneSet
#   The answer rides the laneSet's own documentation, because the laneSet is the partition the
#   search is about.
#
# Defined, not read
#   A program reads these columns, so this statement defines its vocabulary in its own
#   WITH clause: a view keeps the rows and loses the column's origin, which is where
#   NOT NULL lives. assets/sqlc/views.sqlc says what a definition is.
#
-- pm:Stack/pm:couplings/pm:absent; carried as the laneSet's own documentation.
WITH
coupling_searches AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(epistemics/coupling_searches.sqlc)
)
SELECT s.filing,
       coalesce(s.answer::text, 'stated') AS answer
FROM (
    :compose(epistemics/coupling_searches.sqlc)
) s
